// NEGATIVE TEST -- the async PRODUCER (N7-18), which is the plainest spelling
// of `async fn` and was the last one still reaching code generation.
//
// `tests/reject/async_fn.pd` beside this file covers the CONSUMER-shaped case:
// a body carrying a value-returning `return`, refused with a more specific
// diagnostic that arrives first. Neither fixture covers this one, and that is
// exactly why it survived -- measured at acda322, this program compiled clean
// and the emitted C carried
//
//     typedef struct g_Future { int state; } g_Future;
//     int g_poll(g_Future *future) { ... }
//     g_Future g() { ... }
//
// Nothing calls g_poll, so the body never ran either. N7 says effect tracking
// "is entirely static and has no runtime representation"; a struct with a
// `state` field emitted into the program's own C is one, so this was a live
// normative violation rather than a missing feature.
//
// Refused, not silently lowered: dropping the Future struct and compiling the
// body as an ordinary function would leave `async` a keyword the compiler
// ignores. The keyword itself is removed at M5.

async fn g() {
    print("x");
}

fn main() {
    print_int(1);
}
